---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * ⚠️ This file used to describe Google Analytics 4, Cloudflare Web Analytics
 * and AdSense, plus an EEA/UK consent choice. **None of them existed.** It also
 * omitted the one thing the site genuinely stores — the screen calibration.
 *
 * The rule for this page: it describes what the site does TODAY, in the
 * present tense, and nothing else. On a site whose entire pitch is that its
 * numbers are verified rather than copied, a policy listing cookies that are
 * never set is the same failure in a different file. If analytics or ads are
 * added, this page changes in the same commit — not after.
 *
 * ⚠️ That rule was then broken in the other direction. GA4 shipped in 99d303f
 * (20 Sep 2026) and this page was left saying "no analytics", "no Google
 * Analytics" and "no cookies are set" for a day — three false sentences on the
 * one page a reader consults precisely because they do not want to take the
 * rest of the site on trust. Both failures have the same shape: the page
 * described an intention rather than the deployment. Every fact below was read
 * off the live site (cookie names and the 400-day expiry from `cookieStore`,
 * the tag from the rendered `<head>`), not inferred from this repo.
 *
 * ⚠️ And a third time, from outside the repo. From 30 Sep 2026 at the latest,
 * Cloudflare injected its own Web Analytics beacon into every page at the edge
 * (`static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js`, posting to `/cdn-cgi/rum`)
 * while this page said "there is no Cloudflare Web Analytics". Nothing in
 * `src/` or `dist/` contains it — it is switched on in the Cloudflare
 * dashboard — so no code review could have caught it; only reading the SERVED
 * HTML does. What is said about it below was read from the beacon script
 * itself (it reads the page address, the referrer and page-load timings, and
 * references neither cookies nor localStorage) and from Cloudflare's own
 * statement that it does not fingerprint visitors. If the beacon is switched
 * off in the dashboard, that paragraph goes in the same change.
 * QA-AUDIT-2026-09-30.md #1, #13.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, EMAIL, NOINDEX_SITE, GA_MEASUREMENT_ID } from '../config';
import { PPM_STORE_KEY, DPR_STORE_KEY, CAL_OBJECT_STORE_KEY } from '../data/calibration';
import { CONSENT_STORE_KEY, CONSENT_MAX_AGE_DAYS } from '../data/consent';

/* Dated from the commit that made it true, not auto-generated: a policy whose
   date moves on every build tells the reader nothing about when it changed. */
/* ⚠️ The consent banner (feature/consent-banner) makes this page true only
   once it is DEPLOYED. If it ships later than this date, re-date it then. */
const LAST_UPDATED = '6 October 2026';

/* Read from the live cookies rather than from Google's documentation, which
   describes defaults this property may not be using. Both GA4 cookies came
   back at 400 days — the ceiling Chrome caps any cookie at. */
const GA_COOKIE_DAYS = 400;
---
<Layout
  title={`Privacy Policy — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} collects: page views and which ring sizer steps were used, with two analytics cookies only if you accept. Never your measurements.`}
  noindex={NOINDEX_SITE}
>
  <Prose title="Privacy Policy">
    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> {LAST_UPDATED}</p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      There is no account, no sign-up and no advertising on this site today. The ring sizer itself
      runs entirely in your browser: the measurements you type, and the size it works out, are never
      sent anywhere — there is no server here to send them to.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we do collect is ordinary web analytics, and the main part of it only if you say yes.
      Google Analytics 4 records that a page was visited and which steps of the ring sizer were
      used, and sets two cookies to do it — but only after you accept it in the cookie banner. If
      you reject it, or never answer, it does not load at all. Cloudflare, which hosts the site,
      also counts page views and how fast pages load, without cookies, and that part does not
      depend on the banner. That is the whole of it, and both are described in full below.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
    <p>Up to five small values, all in your browser&rsquo;s local storage, none of which leaves it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your theme preference</strong> — light, dark or system, so the page does not flash
        the wrong colour on your next visit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your screen calibration</strong> (<code>{PPM_STORE_KEY}</code>) — a single number,
        how many pixels your display puts in a millimetre, so you do not have to hold a bank card
        against the screen every time.
      </li>
      {/* wrap-anywhere on the key below: at 320 px it is wider than the line
          and pushed the page sideways. */}
      <li>
        <strong>Your browser&rsquo;s zoom level when you calibrated</strong>{' '}
        (<code class="wrap-anywhere">{DPR_STORE_KEY}</code>) — so the sizer can warn you if the zoom has changed since, which
        would make the calibration wrong.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What you calibrated against</strong> (<code>{CAL_OBJECT_STORE_KEY}</code>) — a card, a
        US quarter or a 1 euro coin, so the same one is offered next time. Stored only if you pick one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your answer to the cookie banner</strong> (<code>{CONSENT_STORE_KEY}</code>) — accepted
        or rejected, and when, so you are not asked on every page. It is kept for{' '}
        {CONSENT_MAX_AGE_DAYS} days, about six months; after that the banner asks again.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Clearing this site&rsquo;s data in your browser removes all of them.</p>

    <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This site runs two measurement scripts, and no others: <strong>Google Analytics 4</strong>{' '}
      (measurement ID <code>{GA_MEASUREMENT_ID}</code>), which runs only if you accept it and is
      described first, and Cloudflare Web Analytics, described after it.
    </p>
    <p>It records the ordinary things an analytics tool records:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>which page you opened, when, and which page or search sent you here;</li>
      <li>an approximate location — country, region, roughly the city — worked out from your IP address;</li>
      <li>your device type, browser, operating system and screen size;</li>
      <li>
        engagement on the page: how far you scrolled, outbound link clicks and file downloads.
        Google calls this &ldquo;enhanced measurement&rdquo; and it is switched on.
      </li>
      <li>
        which steps of the ring sizer you took: that you saved a calibration
        and against what (a card, a US quarter or a 1 euro coin); which starting point you chose
        (a ring, your finger, or a measurement you already had); that you first changed the
        measurement, and whether the screen was calibrated at that moment; which measure and unit
        you switched to; and, on the printable sheet, that the print dialog opened and whether from
        the button or the browser&rsquo;s own Print. Each one says a step happened, not what it
        produced.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      It does <strong>not</strong> receive anything you type into the ring sizer. The measurement,
      the calibration and the resulting size stay in your browser; no event carries them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two cookies make this work, both first-party, both set only after you accept, and both
      expiring after {GA_COOKIE_DAYS} days:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>_ga</code> — distinguishes one browser from another.</li>
      <li><code>_ga_{GA_MEASUREMENT_ID.replace('G-', '')}</code> — holds the session state for this property.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing from Google loads until you accept.</strong> Before you answer, and after you
      reject, the Google Analytics script is not fetched at all and neither cookie is set; any left
      over from an earlier visit are deleted. When you accept, the script loads with Google&rsquo;s
      advertising features switched off — Google signals and ad personalisation — and with
      permission for analytics storage only, never advertising storage.
    </p>
    <p>
      To change your mind, use &ldquo;Cookie settings&rdquo; at the foot of any page. Rejecting there
      deletes both cookies and reloads the page so the script stops. Your answer is asked for again
      after about six months. Google&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
        rel="nofollow noopener">opt-out add-on</a> and content blockers also still work, and the ring
      sizer works exactly the same whichever you choose, because it never needed the network.
    </p>

    <h3>Cloudflare Web Analytics</h3>
    <p>
      Cloudflare, which hosts the site, adds its own analytics script to every page. Read from the
      script itself, it reports the address of the page you opened, the page that sent you here, and
      how long the page took to load and respond. It sets no cookies and stores nothing on your
      device, and Cloudflare states that it does not fingerprint visitors by IP address or browser.
      It does not see anything you type into the ring sizer either. We see only totals — page views,
      referrers, countries, device types and load times — never an individual visit. Content blockers stop it too.
      It is not controlled by the cookie banner: Cloudflare adds it at its network edge, outside
      this site&rsquo;s own code, so it runs whatever you choose there.
    </p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>None.</strong> There are no ads and no advertising cookies today. This site is
      intended to carry advertising eventually; when it does, this section will name the network,
      and visitors in the EEA and UK will be asked for consent before any advertising cookie is
      set — not afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by Cloudflare Pages. Like any web host, Cloudflare processes the technical
      details of each request — IP address, browser, and which page was asked for — to deliver the
      page and protect against attacks. That happens at the network level, we do not read it, and
      it is the only third party involved in serving this site. Their handling is covered by <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="nofollow noopener">Cloudflare&rsquo;s privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <p>
      Clear this site&rsquo;s data in your browser to remove the theme and calibration values, and
      the two Google Analytics cookies with them. Cloudflare&rsquo;s analytics stores nothing to clear.
      To stay out of Google Analytics, reject it in the cookie banner or under &ldquo;Cookie
      settings&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we hold is the aggregate Google Analytics reports — visit counts by page, country, device
      and referrer. There is no account, no email and no name attached to any of it, so we cannot
      look you up, and there is nothing personal for us to hand over or correct. If you want a
      visit deleted from those reports, write to us with the approximate date and country and we
      will submit a deletion request to Google; be aware that aggregate counts cannot be unpicked
      per visitor, which is the honest answer rather than a promise we cannot keep.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about any of this: <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a></p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
